1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,686
Before we build an IDM, let us look at what an IDM consists of, and where each part is in the app.

2
00:00:07,586 --> 00:00:18,640
An IDM has five parts: a header, a use case, a process map, exchange requirements with their information units, and rules.

3
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:24,439
ISO 29481-1 defines the method, and ISO 29481-3 the data schema.

4
00:00:25,339 --> 00:00:27,860
The bar on the left opens the parts.

5
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:33,041
The first button opens the IDM header: identifiers, authors and revisions.

6
00:00:33,941 --> 00:00:39,935
The second opens the use case: purpose, uses, region, actor roles, and project phases.

7
00:00:40,835 --> 00:00:48,470
The process map is always in the middle: a BPMN editor with a palette for events, tasks, gateways, data objects,

8
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:49,561
pools and lanes.

9
00:00:50,461 --> 00:00:53,308
The third button opens the exchange requirements.

10
00:00:53,308 --> 00:00:58,887
Select one to see its information units on the right, and select an information unit to edit it.

11
00:00:59,787 --> 00:01:02,731
Rules opens the business rules and constraints.

12
00:01:02,731 --> 00:01:05,675
The same dialog is available from the use case.

13
00:01:06,575 --> 00:01:14,278
Finally, Validate checks the IDM against ISO 29481-3, and Save and Export saves it.

14
00:01:14,278 --> 00:01:17,434
Now let us build an IDM ourselves.

